  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 125 PM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nWV imagery indicates a southwest flow aloft prevailing across the \nWestern High Plains behind weak ridging transitioning east through \nthe Central Plains. Near the surface, a weak lee side trough of low \npressure remains anchored in eastern Colorado.\n\nWidespread shower/thunderstorm chances (70-90%) remain in the \nforecast tonight as the SREF continues to show ridging aloft pushing \nslowly east through the Central Plains, allowing a slowly intensifying\nsouthwest flow aloft across the Southern Rockies to shift gradually\ninto the high plains of eastern Colorado, tapping into monsoonal\nmoisture in the Desert Southwest. Meanwhile, Prevailing southeast\nupsloping will further reinforce low level moisture across the \nregion with surface dewpoints up into the lower 60s(F), providing\nminimal instability. Showers with embedded thunderstorms are likely\nto develop across northeast New Mexico into southeast Colorado this\nevening as an upper level shortwave lifts northeast through the Southern\nRockies into the high plains of eastern Colorado, then spread northeast\ninto southwest Kansas overnight. The best chance for rainfall through\ndaybreak Thursday morning still remains to be across extreme southwest\nKansas as indicated by CAMs and the latest HREF indicating a widespread\n70-90% probability of 24-hr QPF exceeding 0.5 of an inch across much\nof southwest Kansas by late afternoon Thursday. Additional rounds\nof rain (60-80% chance) can be expected Thursday night into Friday\nwith the focus for rainfall shifting east across the remainder of\nsouthwest Kansas into Central Kansas, again with a 70-90% probability\nof 24-hr QPF topping 0.5 of an inch by late Friday morning. \n\nAbove normal temperatures are forecast tonight as prevailing \nsoutheasterlies further draw slightly warmer air into the area, not\nto mention holding surface dewpoints in the upper 50s(F) to near\n60F. With this, look for widespread lows in the 60s(F) with the HREF\nsuggesting a 60-80% probability of temperatures slipping below 65F\nin west central/central Kansas into portions of southwest Kansas with\nless than a 10% probability of dropping below 65F near and along\nthe Oklahoma line. Fairly seasonal temperatures are likely Thursday\nwith scattered showers about and mostly cloudy to overcast skies\nexpected throughout the day. Considering the latest HREF paints a\n70-90% probability of temperatures topping 70F, expect afternoon\nhighs generally in the 70s(F) for much of the area with the lower\n80s(F) still possible in south central Kansas. Similar temperatures\ncan be expected Friday.",
